You can modify the regex pattern to exclude matches that contain `` or `
` characters. One way to do this is to add a negative lookahead assertion to the pattern: ``` (?<=: ")[^"
]* ``` This pattern uses a negative lookahead assertion `[^"
]*` to match one or more characters that are not quotes, carriage returns, or line feeds. Here's an updated example in JavaScript: ```javascript const text = `...your original text...`; const regex = /(?<=: ")[^"
]*/g; const matches = text.match(regex); console.log(matches); // ["Bandit", "HAN-D", "Enforcer", "Bomber", "Beetle"] ``` This should filter out any matches that contain `` or `
` characters. Alternatively, you can also use the `filter()` method to remove matches that contain `` or `
` characters after the fact: ```javascript const text = `...your original text...`; const regex = /(?<=: ")[^"]*/g; const matches = text.match(regex).filter(match => !/|
/.test(match)); console.log(matches); // ["Bandit", "HAN-D", "Enforcer", "Bomber", "Beetle"] ``` This approach can be useful if you need to perform additional filtering or processing on the matches.

